# Signing material. Release keys are never committed, under any name: CI
# writes `apps/mobile/android/keystore.jks` + `key.properties` from secrets
# (flutter_build.yml, fastlane.yml) and Gradle also reads `key-stg.properties`,
# so the patterns are broad on purpose — a keystore named something else is
# still a keystore, and one accidental `git add -A` is all it takes.
#
# The ONE exception is deliberate: `keystore-dev.jks` + `key-dev.properties`
# are tracked (force-added past these patterns) so a fresh clone can build the
# dev flavor. That key is public — anyone can sign with it — and Gradle falls
# back to it for staging/prod when `key-stg.properties` / `key.properties` is
# absent. Never ship a store release with it; see
# docs/en/operations/02_fastlane_release.md §4.
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties
key-*.properties

.cxx
.kotlin
